penditure in the United States reached approximately $650 billion, yet private insurers cannot price algorithmic risk when vendors withhold training provenance and audit logs. Under traditional corporate disclosure standards, commercial markets fail when buyers cannot verify the basic mechanics of what they purchase. If an unmonitored model hallucinates toxic medical dosages or crashes an automated logistics fleet, downstream small businesses bear the direct physical damages. Without mandatory evidence preservation and statutory burden-shifting that forces upstream vendors to open their black-box telemetry in court, private insurance markets simply dissolve into uninsurable legal chaos.\n\nThe International Energy Agency projects that electricity demand from data centers will quadruple by 2030, draining local utility grids while vendor balance sheets capture the financial windfalls. When unmonitored automated systems fail in production, working families and local operators absorb the wreckage while tech giants claim contractual immunity behind standard click-wrap terms of service. Safety does not require speculative hardware kill-switches, and enterprise accountability cannot be abandoned to private insurers operating blind. Congress must establish non-waivable statutory warranties of fitness and mandatory audit logging across the entire enterprise software supply chain. If an upstream technology vendor profits by deploying probabilistic algorithms into critical physical infrastructure, who pays when the machine breaks?",
