onse mechanisms. Deploying unmonitored autonomous agents into electrical grids or hospital systems without automated kill-switches leaves human operators with zero recovery mechanisms during rapid failure cascades.\n\nAccountability requires enforceable legal liability attached directly to the entities that profit from frontier deployment. Commercial vendors who release uncontained weights shift catastrophic operational costs onto regional hospitals, public utilities, and innocent third parties. Regulators must mandate verifiable runtime monitoring and physical execution interlocks as non-negotiable standards of commercial market entry. Human oversight cannot survive as an abstract aspirational policy without the concrete technical authority to halt autonomous execution.",
